infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Primary-key identifier: bytes vs B-tree index locality
  desc The PK is fixed for the table's life — pick for index locality first, then bytes
  lists
    - label BIGSERIAL — 8 bytes, sequential
      desc Auto-incremented integer. Best B-tree locality — new rows append to the rightmost page, minimal fragmentation. Smallest index entry. The right default for single-region services; only limit is the central sequence breaks under multi-region distributed writes.
    - label UUIDv7 — 16 bytes, time-ordered
      desc A 48-bit millisecond timestamp prefix makes values monotonically increasing, so B-tree locality is nearly as good as BIGSERIAL. Doubles the index entry to 16 bytes but buys global uniqueness with no central sequence. The modern default when UUID is needed.
    - label UUIDv4 — 16 bytes, random
      desc Globally unique but each insert hits a random B-tree page, causing page splits and bloat — the index can reach 3-5x the size of a sequential-key index above ~10M rows. Only if uniqueness is required today and UUIDv7 is unavailable.
    - label ULID — 26 bytes, stored as TEXT
      desc Time-sorted and globally unique like UUIDv7, but Postgres has no native ULID type — stored as TEXT at 26 bytes with no native operators. Prefer UUIDv7 in a Postgres shop.
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #6366f1 #f43f5e #f59e0b
